# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — it's completely removed in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone** — no equivalent needed since abort can't happen
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be thrown** — catch blocks for it are dead code

## The Core Problem

Your `Stop()` relies on `Thread.Abort()` to force-kill a blocked thread. In .NET 8, you must use **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful exit with a timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't finish in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO safe force-kill equivalent; fix GetNextItem to respect the token.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Blocking calls that ignore the token** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on I/O or a `ManualResetEvent` that never checks the token, cancellation will hang indefinitely. You *must* thread the token through to all blocking calls. |
| **No force-kill replacement** | There is no safe equivalent to `Thread.Abort()`. Design for cooperative cancellation or use a separate process if you need hard termination. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `volatile` flag can't interrupt a blocked `Read()` or `WaitOne()`. Only a `CancellationToken` (which signals `WaitHandle`) can unblock waits. |
| **Thread.Sleep → Task.Delay** | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with `await Task.Delay(duration, token)` so delays are cancellable. |
| **Exception semantics differ** | `ThreadAbortException` auto-rethrew after each catch. `OperationCanceledException` is a normal exception — handle it once and you're done. |

## If `GetNextItem` Uses a BlockingCollection

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework):
var item = _queue.Take(); // only interruptible via Thread.Abort

// After (.NET 8):
var item = _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
```

## If You Need Hard Timeout Guarantees

If the worker truly cannot be made cooperative (e.g., calling unmanaged code), isolate it in a **separate process** and kill the process on timeout. In-process `Thread.Abort` was always unsafe — .NET 8 just makes that explicit.
